an to imply that that their  position is sort of timeless and  unchanging so I think of traditional  Chiefs as Chiefs who have power by  virtue of their association with the  customs of their community so  they're popularly thought of being  related to tradition but this doesn't  mean that position hasn't changed over  time in fact during the colonial period  there you know the positions of these  leaders did change greatly the trip the  colonial state recognized some leaders  pre-existing leaders and they didn't  recognize others they generally changed  their powers during this time period and  in fact traditional she's got quite a  bad reputation in the late colonial  period so that the independence era  leaders for the most part were had very  negative attitudes towards them at worst  they viewed them as traders who have  been complicit in the colonial project  and at best they just sort of viewed  them as these sort of anachronistic  leaders who they thought would sort of  fade away into the sunset with time but  fast forward you know at to the last 20  years and actually we see that  traditional Chiefs still exist across  much of Africa and at the local level  they still are very important actors so  if they run run court systems they often  play an important role in allocating  land and they play a really important  role in organizing community  contributions to local projects so we  might think of this kind of as  organizing informal taxes so if a  community needs to come together and  build a school or to maintain a road  it's typically the chief who will fund  raise or organize the community to  provide free labor for the project and  so their Chiefs know they exist across  much of sub-saharan Africa and what's  more they seem to be more important at  the local level in the more democratic  countries and this is a little bit you  know why would this be but to take a  step back and think about it in the post  and independence period there were very  few elected governments in Africa and  what that actually meant was that for  the most part politicians could ignore  the the plight of rural citizens the  rural majority could be ignored because  these politicians weren't facing  elections the introduction of  competitive elections means all of a  sudden these leaders need to figure out  a way to win votes sugar rural voters  and what do rural voters want the  product gets very basic things you know  what they want schools and clinics and  roads and wells there is the state is so  weak in so much of rural Africa that  these elected politicians don't have a  mechanism for responding to to these  demands on their own there are state  bureaucracies they can rely on to to to  organize these projects elected leaders  could try to go to these communities and  initiate things by themselves but it  turns out that's also difficult for them  they have obligations perhaps in the  capital city that they need to attend to  and they elected leaders are generally  in office for only a short period of  time so they don't necessarily have the  local level institutions that they can  use to organize communities to provide  projects and so this is where  traditional leaders come in and they  actually pay a really important role in  facilitating the work of elected leaders  especially in so far as a lot of  resources and projects the way that  they're provided in in rural Africa is  through what co-produced co-production  so the central state provide some  resources and the communities are also  expected to provide some resources for  the projects and so the organization of  the community labor or the community  fundraising and General Electric  auditions aren't that effective at doing  that on their own this is where they  turn to traditional cheeks and  traditional cheese can help them out  okay our traditional Chiefs given any  kind of compensation for their role in  the community um so that really varies  widely across my country I think 