e of the  challenges I had you know in trying to  develop this research design was I  developing a design that both allowed me  sort of to understand complexity of  their roles while also being able to  generalize across cases so the way i did  this is sort of through a multi-tiered  approach so at the narrowest and deepest  level my book project builds on research  that was done at two sites in zambia one  in the northern province and one in the  eastern province and in Zambia is a  central african country it's very poor  it's landlocked it has a ghastly strong  history of competitive elections so  since nineteen  d 1 there have been numerous  presidential and parliamentary elections  and actually there's the government  turnover so we have competitive  elections at the national level but in  rural Zambia there's still you know  almost 300 Chiefs who have quite a bit  of insolence at the local level and I'm  sorry to interrupt is there one chief /  town for instance so Chiefs um on  average you can think of them as  controlling territories that are much  bigger than towns but the populations  with believing at 10,000 you know and so  they're geographically designated in  Zambia and so that these two research  sites and again so it was in the eastern  and the northern region of the province  of the country and I conducted extensive  research so I you know did household  surveys interviews with the chief I  spend a lot of time in those communities  I have my own observations and then but  from there I wanted to be able to you  know reach out and generalize beyond  that so in three provinces of Zambia I  conducted a comprehensive interviews  with all of the traditional chiefs and  those three provinces there are seven  rural provinces in database that was  about half of them and I interviewed a  large proportion of the elected  politicians from those provinces to then  I want to generalize a little beyond  that too so I relied on data from the  ministries in in the capital of Zambia  lusaka and the archives to create a data  set that actually covers all of the  Chiefs in in Zambia from the period  which competitive elections were  reintroduced in 1991 to the present and  and then finally I wanted to be able to  see something beyond Zambia too and so I  also test some of the implications of  the theory I developed cross nationally  using data that we've collected by other  people for the most part in other  countries okay so let's talk a little  bit about the the broader implications  so um I think at the so  the research shows that in many ways the  role of Chiefs is more positive than  many people have suggested and so I  think there's sort of two important  findings that come out of the research  for the book so first I think one of  those important findings is actually  sort of a negative finding many people  have thought that traditional Chiefs can  deliver voters on blocked whichever  political party they choose which would  be really damaging for democratic  accountability would sort of suggest  that voters can be too you know forced  to vote for whoever the chief prefers  without any consideration of their own  interest and actually the research  suggests that that's not the case you  know for the most part people in  understand that their vote is secret  they may feel obliged to obey the Chiefs  instructions in certain realms but they  don't necessarily think they have to  obey them in the political realm so I  want to have that sort of a very  positive thing for democratic  accountability second as I already  alluded to these cheese's play a really  important role in facilitating  democratic accountability in so far as  they sort of help elected politicians  implement programming at the very local  level in community so this is sort of  like central paradox of the book these  unelected leaders facilitate the work of  elected leaders then but nope there are  also there there are some cons as well  for generational change and so obviously  the situation where electric politicians  need to rely on these unelected  traditional Chiefs to implement local  programming is not ideal compared to a  situation where they could instead rely  on a neutral meritocratic bureaucracy  but in the short term that's not an  option so I don't think that you know  that criticism I think we can push aside  at least in the short term a more  serious criticism is because traditional  chief sort of provide the stopgap or  almost band-aid solution allowing  politicians to deliver resources to  rural communities without a strong  bureaucracy they prevent they reduce the  incentive to invest in  lengthening bureaucracy and in the long  run that might actually be a much more  effective way it probably would be a  more effective way of delivering these  resources and providing programming to  people in rural Africa so so you know  they sort of this short term long term  trade-off here okay well very  fascinating stuff I have to say thank  you so much for being here today and  sharing center of your work thank you  very much for having me for more  information about professor Baldwin  enter work please visit our website at  yale edu slash mcmillan report be sure  to join us again for another episode of  the macmillan report made possible  through funding from the Whitney and  Betty Macmillan Center for International  and area studies at Yale  you

